  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1237 AM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nSaturday through Monday...An expansive upper level ridge will remain \ncentered over Colorado. H85 temperatures will range as high as 34 to \n38C each day. Hot temperatures are expected. NBM probability of \nexceeding 100 degrees is highest on Sunday from 70 to 95 percent \nacross much of north central, the western Sandhills and southwest. \nEven Saturday has probabilities as high as 40 to 70 percent. \nAlthough confidence in exceeding 100 degree is less on Monday, a \nweak cold may drop into the forecast area. This may actually bring \nthe hottest day if the front slows and compressional heating boosts \nhighs. MEX guidance is hot all 3 days, at 108, 110, 108 each day at \nValentine and 106, 109, 110 each day at North Platte. While we may \nnot see highs quite this hot, the potential is there for highs to \nreach or exceed 105 in some locations. Dewpoints during peak heating \nlook to range from the upper 50s to lower 60s, with heat indicies at \nleast in the upper 90s to low 100s. Sunday may see heat indices 100 \nto 105. So, there is medium confidence than heat advisories may be \nneeded. \n\nTuesday and Wednesday...Highs may lower a bit Tuesday into the low \nto mid 90s, as an upper trough moves across the Great Lakes with a \nweak cold front down into northern Kansas. Low to mid 90s also \nexpected for highs on Wednesday.\n\nDry conditions expected through Sunday, with low chances for showers \nand storms Monday afternoon through Wednesday.  H7 temperatures \naround 18 to around 20C Saturday warm to around 20C Sunday into \nMonday. Slightly cooler temperatures aloft Tuesday and Wednesday and \na slightly northwest flow aloft may support \"ridge runner \nconvection\" into the evening and overnight hours.",
